---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="The AI-Powered Engineer">
    <article>
        <header>
            <figure>
                <div>
                    <img src="/blog/ai_powered/aipowered_intro.png" alt="The AI-Powered Engineer Cover Image"
                         loading="lazy">
                </div>
            </figure>

            <h1 dir="ltr">
                <span> The AI-Powered Engineer</span>
            </h1>
        </header>
        <div>
            <div>
                <div>
                    <time>
                        September 16, 2026
                    </time>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div>
                    <div dir="ltr">

                        <div>
                            <p>
                                Welcome to the next era. If you read the headlines, you might think the software
                                engineering profession is on its deathbed, ready to be entirely handed over to AI.
                                As a software engineer who works with these tools every day, I’m here to tell you:
                                <strong>take a deep breath. </strong>AI is not here to replace us
                                <em>(at least for the near future)</em>. It is here <strong>to supercharge
                                us</strong>.
                                Based on the reality of day-to-day development, here is why the human engineer is
                                still
                                the most critical part of the stack - and why learning to work effectively with AI
                                will become an essential engineering skill.
                            </p>

                            <h3>
                                The "End of Juniors" is a Myth
                            </h3>

                            <div>
                                <figure>
                                    <img src="/blog/ai_powered/juniors.png" loading="lazy" alt="Junior">
                                </figure>
                            </div>

                            <p>
                                There is a massive fear that companies will stop hiring junior developers. While the
                                sheer
                                volume of junior hires might adjust, the hiring pipeline won't disappear.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>The Legacy Pipeline</strong>: Someone has to support, maintain, and
                                eventually
                                architect the complex infrastructures of tomorrow.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>The Knowledge Gap</strong>: If you don't train juniors today, you won't have
                                the senior engineers required to untangle massive, legacy AI-generated codebases in
                                five years.
                                We still need humans in the loop, learning the ropes.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>"It Works" vs. "It Works Very Well"</strong>
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Even the best AI models today excel at writing code that just works. But software
                                engineering is rarely about getting a script to run; it's about building optimized,
                                highly performant, and scalable systems. Under the guidance of a skillful engineer,
                                AI accelerates development tremendously. Left to its own devices, it might build a
                                functional but deeply unoptimized mess. The magic happens when human expertise meets
                                machine speed.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>What You Ask Is What You Get</strong>
                            </p>

                            <div>
                                <figure>
                                    <img src="/blog/ai_powered/makeitwork.png" loading="lazy" alt="Make it work">
                                </figure>
                            </div>

                            <p>
                                Let's look at a standard software development lifecycle: it starts with an idea,
                                requirements,
                                moves to design and documentation, then planning, implementation, and finally
                                testing. Today,
                                we can use AI at every single one of these steps. But this is exactly where the trap
                                lies -
                                many people leave too much to the AI without <strong>verifying what is actually
                                received</strong>.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                If a Product Owner provides a perfectly defined, meticulously described task, the AI
                                can
                                generate a solid feature. But let's be real about our day-to-day practice:
                            </p>

                            <blockquote>
                                In practice, many tickets arrive with incomplete requirements, have generic
                                descriptions
                                ("Just do something useful and working").
                            </blockquote>

                            <p>
                                When we skip human verification, the whole lifecycle suffers. You end up with a mess
                                of
                                unclear documentation, missing architectural gaps, and highly generic tickets
                                <strong>about everything and nothing.</strong>
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                If you pass an AI-generated, vague ticket into an AI-coding assistant, you know
                                exactly
                                what happens: an endless loop of iterations, zero clarity, and a questionable final
                                product.
                                &nbsp;AI can write brilliant code - if the input is brilliant. Human translation of
                                business
                                needs is still irreplaceable.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>AI Doesn't Understand Responsibility</strong>
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                You can delegate practically any coding or non-coding task to an AI, but you
                                <strong>can’t delegate the responsibility.</strong> When a critical bug hits
                                production,
                                or a database goes down at 5 AM, someone has to answer for it. You cannot blame the
                                machine;
                                it merely executed orders. Accountability will always fall on a person because
                                humans carry
                                the risk. Most organizations need humans to own decisions and their consequences.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>From Weeks to Days</strong>
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Recently, I had to design a solution for one of my projects. As usual, the first
                                part
                                of the work was architecture and documentation. I needed to produce items like:
                                &nbsp;High-Level
                                Design (HLD), Low-Level Design (LLD), Technical explanations, Architectural
                                decisions,
                                Implementation details, etc.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                I used AI to help generate all of this, but it wasn't a magic button where I entered
                                one
                                prompt and received perfect documents. There were many iterations. I had to explain
                                the context,
                                correct its assumptions, change the architecture when it missed the mark. Ask for
                                alternatives,
                                remove things that didn't make sense <em>(maybe ~40% of the work)</em>, add missing
                                details.
                                Thoroughly review everything - and repeat.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Even with all of that back-and-forth, it was much faster than writing everything
                                from
                                scratch myself. And that's exactly how AI should be used.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>The implementation:</strong> The standard expectation was to hand the design
                                over to
                                the PO, spend weeks with a team splitting it into tickets, estimating story points,
                                and deciding
                                on the implementation order.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Instead, I decided to experiment: I created <strong>just one story linked to all the
                                documents</strong>
                                and used AI to implement the solution.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>The Complexity</strong>: It wasn't the simplest feature, but not the most
                                complex either.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>The Result:</strong> The entire solution was done in a couple of days. Based
                                on my
                                experience with comparable features, I estimate that this approach was roughly 3-5x
                                faster.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>Quality Factor:</strong> This is probably the most important part. I didn't
                                simply
                                accept whatever the AI generated. There were iterations, mistakes, and things that
                                had to be
                                redesigned. The code was reviewed, tests were verified, and best practices were
                                enforced
                                through our normal PR review process.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <em>- Did it work perfectly on the first try? - of course it didn't.</em> But after
                                a few
                                iterations under my supervision, we had a clean, maintainable solution ready for
                                production.
                            </p>

                            <h3>
                                One Person, More Capabilities
                            </h3>

                            <p>
                                I have a close friend who is an indie developer. Ask him what he does and you won't
                                get a
                                one-line answer. He's the only engineer behind a brand with several consumer
                                products: desktop
                                customization tools, wallpapers, browser games. Everything technical goes through
                                him. And
                                <strong>"everything technical" covers a lot.</strong>
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Each product is its own codebase, with its own users and its own problems. Around
                                them there's
                                infrastructure, websites, SEO, payments, analytics, support tooling. And on top of
                                that, a bunch
                                of other projects that have nothing to do with the brand at all.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Building was never the blocker for him — <strong>time was.</strong> Frontend,
                                backend, DevOps,
                                across several products, plus keeping everything already shipped alive, is more work
                                than one
                                person's week can hold. So things waited. Or he had to find another engineer to
                                help.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Now, with AI, that has changed. From what I see, he's doing much more of it himself
                                and doing
                                it well. AI takes on implementation, debugging, exploring unfamiliar territory,
                                documentation,
                                and the long tail of small tasks that used to need another pair of hands.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                It doesn't replace him. Someone still has to understand the system, decide what to
                                build and how,
                                judge whether the generated code is any good, and own it when it breaks in
                                production. But the
                                engineering effort it takes to move an idea forward has dropped. For a one-engineer
                                company,
                                <strong>that's the difference between "someday" and "this week."</strong>
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>One person can now accomplish what previously might have required several
                                    people or at
                                    least additional engineering support.</strong>
                            </p>

                            <h2>
                                Wait... This Article Was Built the Same Way
                            </h2>

                            <div>
                                <figure>
                                    <img src="/blog/ai_powered/aiask.png" loading="lazy" alt="Help of AI">
                                </figure>
                            </div>

                            <p>
                                And there is a funny part about writing this article.&nbsp; Even now, while you're
                                reading it,
                                it took me a few iterations to get the right phrasing and structure. I had to
                                clarify my points,
                                correct assumptions, and refine the flow. <em>(You might even see the typos in my
                                first attempt
                                above, and yeah, it’s just before the Future)</em>
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                I didn't just throw raw thoughts into an AI and publish whatever came back. AI
                                pointed out
                                unclear sections, generated images and helped structure my thoughts.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                However, the final article isn't just "AI-generated text." It is the result of my
                                <strong>personal</strong> <strong>experiences</strong>, my
                                <strong>decisions</strong>, and
                                careful <strong>manual review.</strong> AI didn't replace me. It helped me turn my
                                raw
                                ideas into a well-structured, readable article. And that is the core point of this
                                <strong>article</strong>.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <strong>The Future Belongs to Engineers Who Adapt</strong>
                            </p>

                            <div>
                                <figure>
                                    <img src="/blog/ai_powered/future.png" loading="lazy" alt="Future">
                                </figure>
                            </div>

                            <p>
                                Let’s be realistic: because AI allows us to do more with fewer resources, companies
                                may
                                need fewer developers to deliver the same amount of software. Team sizes might
                                shrink,
                                and expectations will certainly change. To stay competitive and valuable, developers
                                will
                                need to learn <strong>how to work effectively with AI</strong>.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                For at least the next couple of years, people will remain at the center of software
                                development -
                                supported by increasingly powerful AI tools. We shouldn't fear this shift. Every
                                developer
                                should treat AI like any other engineering tool:
                            </p>

                            <ul>
                                <li>Learn it.</li>
                                <li>Experiment with it.</li>
                                <li>Understand its limitations.</li>
                                <li>Learn how to provide rich context and review its output.</li>
                                <li>Learn when to trust it - and, more importantly, <strong>when NOT to trust
                                    it.</strong></li>
                            </ul>

                            <p>
                                The future is cooperation between the human who makes the decisions and the AI that
                                helps
                                implement them. Developers who know what to ask, how to provide context, and how to
                                guide
                                and verify the machine's output will have a significant advantage in an AI-assisted
                                development environment.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                And this isn't just my experience. While discussing a draft of this article with the
                                same
                                friend, he sent me a link to a livestream where <a target="_self" tabindex="0"
                                                                                   href="http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zcLPGC-tvgk"
                                                                                   data-test-app-aware-link=""><strong>Matt
                                Pocock interviewed Uncle Bob</strong></a>
                                on software fundamentals in the age of AI. I hadn't seen it before. Watching it
                                genuinely
                                surprised me - so many of the ideas they discussed were very close to the
                                conclusions
                                I'd already reached from my own daily work.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Another interesting example is <strong>Mitchell Hashimoto</strong> - co-founder of
                                HashiCorp,
                                co-creator of Terraform, and the developer behind Ghostty. He is a heavy user of AI
                                in his
                                development workflow. In his essay <a target="_self" tabindex="0"
                                                                      href="https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey"
                                                                      data-test-app-aware-link=""><strong>My AI
                                Adoption Journey</strong></a>,
                                he describes how AI has become deeply integrated into his workflow while engineering
                                fundamentals,
                                architectural thinking, and reviewing the result remain essential.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Whether you look at industry legends like Uncle Bob or experienced builders like
                                Mitchell,
                                the takeaway is clear: AI changes how we build software, but it doesn't remove the
                                need for
                                engineers who understand how it should be built.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Remember: <strong>Garbage In → Garbage Out</strong>.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                With AI, this principle has never been more important. <strong>And I think that's
                                the real future of software engineering.</strong>
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </article>
    <style>
        h1 {
            text-align: center !important;
        }

        figure {
            text-align: center;
        }

        figcaption {
            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 8px;
            font-style: italic;
        }
    </style>
</Layout>
